  "query_preview": "..\n\nA broad northern US ridge and a closed low under it over west \nTexas define the upper level pattern this morning, with water \nvapor imagery depicting impulses near the closed low moving into\nthe desert southwest from the east. An elongated swath of rich \natmospheric moisture sits from Sonora north through much of the\nIntermountain West, characterized by precipitable water values \n1.25 inches near the southern Arizona and New Mexico border to 2\ninches in western Pima county. The combination of the dynamics \naloft and moisture rich environment will allow for widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms capable of 2 inch per hour \n(likely even higher with the strongest storms) rainfall rates to\ndevelop this afternoon across the region. Some areas, such as \nwest of Tucson, may not see thunderstorms until the evening, but\nthe risk of flash flooding covers most of southeastern Arizona \ntoday. A Flash Flood Watch remains in effect. Though there has \nbeen some shifting around in recent high-resolution model runs, \nthe overall suite of model precipitation forecasts generally \nfavor the heaviest rainfall totals to land from southern Pinal \nthrough central- eastern Pima, western Cochise, and through \nSanta Cruz counties where 1-3 inches of rain are possible (with \nisolated potential for more). WPC has extended their Day 1 \nModerate Risk of excessive rainfall and flash flooding (already\na rare outlook level for this area) through the area covered by\nthis model agreement. Flash flooding and running washes and \nrivers will be hazards today, as well as a gusty wind and \nblowing dust threat with any stronger thunderstorm. With the\ndynamic support aloft from the nearby closed low, isolated to\nscattered thunderstorm activity may linger well into the \novernight hours.\n\nThe upper level pattern and moisture field on Friday will be\nsimilar to today, however atmospheric instability is in \nquestion as activity from today will impact Friday's\nthunderstorm potential. This is shown in the model suite where \npossible outcomes range from a notable \"down\" day to a \nscattered flash flooding threat for the area. \n\nThunderstorm chances continue daily through the coming week with\nday to day variability. Ensemble models show the upper level \nridge becoming centered again over or near the Four Corners \nregion by the end of next week with the overall pattern still \nfavoring southeasterly flow into Arizona with continued monsoon \nactivity.",
